930:
The purpose and intent of the tree preservation and landscape ordinance is to establish policies, regulations and standards necessary to ensure that the City will continue to realize the benefits provided by its urban forest. The provisions of this chapter are enacted to:
(a) Provide standards for the preservation of trees, on public and private lands in the City, as part of the land development and building construction process;
(b) Prevent massive grading of land, both developed and undeveloped, without provision for replacement of trees;
(c) Protect designated trees from damage during construction;
(d) Ensure that a minimum density of trees, in a diversity of species and age classes, is distributed and maintained on all developed sites to maximize the benefits of a stable urban forest; and
(e) Provide guidelines for installation and maintenance of trees through good arboricultural practices.
(Code 1990, § 8-10-4; Ord. No. 638, § 1, Exh. A, 10-4-2000)

932:
(a) No land disturbance permit shall be issued until the Director determines that the proposed development is in conformance with the provisions of this chapter. (Refer to Ord. No. 638, Appendix C, Figure 1 - Flowchart of approval process.)
(b) When a person applies for a land disturbance permit, such person shall also apply for a tree removal permit. The complete application must include required plans and supporting documents.
(c) All required plans shall be drawn to scale (one inch equals 50 feet or larger), prepared and sealed by a State registered landscape architect, registered land surveyor, or registered professional engineer.
(d) All required plans shall include the following information:
(1) Name of the owner/developer, and a 24 hour contact name and phone number;
(2) Name of the development;
(3) North point and scale;
(4) Tract boundary lines;
(5) Parcel dimensions (bearings and distances);
(6) Topographic information at two-foot contour intervals;
(7) Parcel zoning districts and all abutting zoning districts;
(8) Reference points to at least two permanent monuments;
(9) Existing buildings, building sites and/or lots;
(10) Means of ingress and egress, and proposed street system;
(11) Proposed dedications for streets, and other public facilities;
(12) Existing easements and rights-of-way.
(Code 1990, § 8-10-6; Ord. No. 638, § 1, Exh. A, 10-4-2000)

944:
(a) Modifications to the tree preservation and tree replacement plan, and subsequently to the landscape development plan requirements of this chapter which are found not to be contrary to the public interest and without which a literal enforcement of the landscape provisions would result in unnecessary and undue hardship may be permitted by the Director, provided that the modification meets the intent of the chapter and is a relocation, not an overall reduction, of the landscape requirements within the property.
(b) Requests for modification shall be submitted in writing to the Director for consideration with an explanation of hardship and how the plan with modifications meets the intent of the chapter.
(c) Modifications may be sought for the following:
(1) Species selection: additional species that share characteristics of species listed may be proposed for equivalent credit;
(2) Percent of shade trees in the site perimeter planting strip, provided a clear conflict with existing utilities or some other existing site conflicts are present;
(3) Width and dimensions of minimum on-site planting areas:
(A) Perimeter planting strips may be an average ten-foot width;
(B) Landscape medians may be an average eight-foot width;
(C) Reduction in planting area for each tree planted may be approved if appropriate soil mix to a depth of 18 inches is achieved; however, in no instance may the planting area be less than 150 square feet for each tree, with a minimum dimension of eight feet in one direction;
(4) Percent trees of one species;
(5) Percent native trees proposed;
(6) Screening, buffer, and yard requirements as set forth in Chapter 7 of this title, the zoning regulations, provided buffers between incompatible uses shall provide adequate opaque visual screening and the slope of such buffer shall not exceed four to one;
(7) Other requirements (i.e., dimension and/or number of parking spaces) as set forth in the zoning chapter;
(8) Other modifications specifically provided for within this chapter.
(d) Under no circumstances shall modification(s) reduce the calculated required tree density units. In addition, the Director does not have the authority to approve a reduction in the percent of tree density credit units required to be planted on the subject property. These and other similar requests require the approval of the Board of Zoning Appeals and Adjustments through a variance approval process.
(Code 1990, § 8-10-15; Ord. No. 638, § 1, Exh. A, 10-4-2000)

